'' DIM SHARED lives in MODULE storage, and the point of the test is that two procedures
'' see ONE variable. Before this, LocalFor matched by name within the current function,
'' so every procedure allocated its own private copy and a SUB assigning a shared
'' variable updated nothing anyone else could see -- silently, with no diagnostic.
''
'' Measured from fbc (docs/internals/phase-6-shared.md):
''   dim shared as long H = 5   ->  static int32 H$ = 5;      (a static INITIALISER)
''   dim shared as string T     ->  static FBSTRING T$;       (no memset, no fb_StrInit)
'' TEST: cli
'' ARGS: --dump-ir tests/mir/src/shared.bas
'' EXPECT-EXIT: 0
'' EXPECT-OUT: verify: ok
'' EXPECT-OUT: 0 scope imbalance

'' The initialiser is part of the DEFINITION, the way fbc emits 'static int32 H$ = 5'.
'' It is not a store executed in main.
'' EXPECT-OUT: global var i32 counter = 5
'' EXPECT-OUT: global var agg name_ size 24

'' Both procedures address the SAME storage. If these were frame slots each would say
'' @counter instead, and the test would still look reasonable -- which is exactly how
'' the bug survived.
'' EXPECT-OUT: store @@counter
'' EXPECT-OUT: call.rtl ptr fb_StrConcatAssign(@@name_

'' No procedure allocates a local for either of them.
'' EXPECT-NOT: local i32 counter
'' EXPECT-NOT: alloca ptr counter

'' A shared string gets NO cleanup: fbc emits no fb_StrDelete for one anywhere, and
'' registering one would release module storage at the end of a scope.
'' EXPECT-NOT: fb_StrDelete(@@name_
'' EXPECT-NOT: <NO TERMINATOR>
'' GOLDEN: expected/shared.out
